WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.735
Use-after-free is a lifetime safety defect.

2
00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:10.941
It happens when code accesses an object, memory region, pointer, reference, iterator, or view after the object behind that access is no longer alive.

3
00:00:10.941 --> 00:00:16.069
The name often makes people think only of explicit delete , but the pattern is broader.

4
00:00:16.069 --> 00:00:20.856
Any stale access after valid lifetime has ended can create unreliable and unsafe behavior.

5
00:00:20.856 --> 00:00:23.933
In C++, stale access can come from many places.

6
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:27.694
A raw pointer can remain after an owner destroys the object.

7
00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:31.113
A reference can outlive the local variable it referred to.

8
00:00:31.113 --> 00:00:34.874
A container operation can invalidate iterators or pointers into its storage.

9
00:00:34.874 --> 00:00:38.634
A std::string_view can survive longer than the string it views.

10
00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:43.763
A callback or background task can run after the object it captured has been destroyed.

11
00:00:43.763 --> 00:00:47.182
The defensive concern is that the code still looks ordinary.

12
00:00:47.182 --> 00:00:49.233
The pointer may be non-null.

13
00:00:49.233 --> 00:00:51.627
The reference may have a familiar name.

14
00:00:51.627 --> 00:00:54.362
The iterator may still compare like a value.

15
00:00:54.362 --> 00:00:57.097
None of that proves the object is valid.

16
00:00:57.097 --> 00:01:02.225
Safe engineering requires reasoning about object lifetime, not just whether an address or handle exists.

17
00:01:02.225 --> 00:01:05.302
Attackers and production failures both benefit from confusion here.

18
00:01:05.302 --> 00:01:14.192
Freed storage may be reused for different data, so a stale access can read the wrong object, corrupt a new object, or drive control flow through

19
00:01:14.192 --> 00:01:16.243
assumptions that are no longer true.

20
00:01:16.243 --> 00:01:19.662
Preventing use-after-free improves reliability, maintainability, and security review.

21
00:01:19.662 --> 00:01:23.765
The goal is not to memorize every possible invalidation rule in isolation.

22
00:01:23.765 --> 00:01:31.971
The goal is to design code where ownership is explicit, borrowed access is short-lived, cleanup is predictable, and later work cannot quietly use

23
00:01:31.971 --> 00:01:33.680
objects that have already ended.
